Source package: gnome-shell

no-screenshots-while-locked.patch
    Hides the screenshot button in quick settings while the screen is locked, and makes Print, which
    opens the screenshot UI, and Shift+Print, which saves a screenshot of the whole screen, do nothing
    there. GNOME Shell offers them on the lock screen on purpose, as its maintainer confirmed in
    https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/gnome-shell/-/issues/9469: the lock screen shows nothing private,
    and screen recording, selecting a window and Alt+Print are already not offered while locked.
    Anyone at the locked machine could still save screenshots into ~/Pictures/Screenshots and replace
    the clipboard's contents with them. Upstream keeps its behavior, so this patch stays.

    The InteractiveScreenshot method of org.gnome.Shell.Screenshot, through which the screenshot
    portal lets an application ask the user for a screenshot, is left as it is and still opens the
    screenshot UI while locked.

    The keybindings are added in the constructor of ScreenshotUI in js/ui/screenshot.js, where
    restrictedModes already leaves out the lock screen for screen recording and Alt+Print, and the
    button is ScreenshotItem in js/ui/status/system.js. After a version bump, check that nothing else
    opening the screenshot UI was added with action modes which include the lock screen.

    The patch adds no test. The build runs gnome-shell's tests, which start the whole shell too, but
    none of them locks the screen or opens the screenshot UI.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with the screen locked:

      quick settings     should show no screenshot button, and show it again after unlocking
      Print              should open no screenshot UI
      Shift+Print        should save no new file into ~/Pictures/Screenshots

leave-scope-to-glib.patch
    Lets GNOME Shell leave the scope of an application launched from the dash, the overview or a
    search to GLib, when the platform data of GLib's "launched" signal names one as "systemd-unit".
    The GLib of patches/glib2.0/ puts the application into that scope before it starts, and a
    request for the scope afterwards would only fail, with a warning in the journal. Drop the patch
    together with patches/glib2.0/scope-before-exec.patch.

    The request is made in shell_global_app_launched_cb in src/shell-global.c. The patch adds no
    test.

spawn-commands-in-scope.patch
    Starts a command run with Alt+F2, or by Util.spawn() and the other spawn helpers of
    js/misc/util.js, in its systemd scope, as the GLib of patches/glib2.0/ starts applications.
    trySpawn spawned the command with Shell.util_spawn_async() and asked for its scope only once it
    ran, so every process the command started in the meantime stayed in GNOME Shell's cgroup and was
    stopped with it. The command is now launched like an application, by the new
    shell_util_spawn_command_async() in src/shell-util.c, through a GAppInfo built from its command
    line, with arguments quoted where needed and % escaped, as it starts a field code in the Exec
    key. The rlimit nofile is reset in the child as before, and the child stays a child of the
    Shell, which reaps it, as polkit relies on. Ubuntu's check for snaps moved with the scope
    request into shell_global_app_launched_cb, which handles the request now when GLib did not
    create a scope. Drop the patch together with patches/glib2.0/scope-before-exec.patch.

    The patch adds no test to GNOME Shell's, which do not run commands, but patches/glib2.0/tests/
    launches a command line the way shell_util_spawn_command_async() does, in its scenario
    command-line.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login: run sh -c 'sleep 600 & exec sleep 601' with
    Alt+F2, and both sleeps should be in app-gnome-sh-<pid>.scope, while systemd-cgls --user-unit
    org.gnome.Shell@ubuntu.service should list no sleep.

disconnect-app-signals-on-destroy.patch
    Backport of GNOME Shell merge request 4491, which is not merged yet. The app grid, the search view
    and the date menu's events and world clocks sections connect to the installed-changed signal of
    Shell.AppSystem, and the search view to the app-filter-changed signal of the parental controls
    manager, and never disconnect. At shutdown the layout manager destroys their actors, and the time
    limits manager then runs a nested main loop, in which a late installed-changed reaches the
    destroyed actors. GJS then logs "has been already disposed" criticals, which fail the build's
    closeWithActiveWindows test now and then, and with it the build. The handlers are connected with
    connectObject() now, so they are disconnected with their actors. Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships
    a GNOME Shell with the merge request.
